package ast

// Package ast defines the syntax tree of a Tack source file, as the
// parser builds it. It is a port of the Go compiler's package ast, as far
// as the parser and the --ast dump need it so far: the nodes and what the
// parser puts in them, and the dump. The fields later passes fill in, the
// resolver's Def, the type checker's types and constants, are not here
// yet; they come with the passes that set them, and the dump writes them
// at the zero values the parser leaves them at in Go. Go's Walk and the
// helpers the later passes call are not ported yet either.
//
// The parser produces a File from the lexer's tokens: the package
// clause's name, the imports and top-level declarations in source order,
// and the comments. A File owns its tree. Go's tree is pointers to nodes
// of many types; Tack has no pointers, and a type may hold an id or a
// span of itself, so here every node is a Node in the File's nodes
// arena, named by an id[Node], and holds the ids of its children. A
// Node is a Kind, a union with one case for each type of node Go has,
// and a span; the id 0, the arena's sentinel, of Kind none, stands for
// Go's nil. A node's lists, its parameters or its statements, are Nodes,
// runs of the lists arena, and its names and strings runs of the bytes
// arena, so every Kind is copyable: a pass copies a node's kind out,
// changes it and writes it back where Go sets a field through a pointer.
// A Nodes records whether it was made, which tells Go's nil slice, null
// in the dump, from its empty one, [] (see Nodes). Func and Param, the
// largest payloads, are kept in arenas of their own.
//
// Go's node types fall into three sealed interfaces, Decl, Stmt and Expr;
// here the family of a node follows from its kind. CaseClause, Field and
// UnionCase, parts of their Switch, StructType and UnionType in Go, are
// nodes here, so the lists holding them are Nodes like any other.
//
// Spans are source.Span, which the port keeps in package source: a file,
// as an index into the compilation's source.Set, and byte offsets. Go's
// zero Span, which names the file "", is unset_span here, FileID -1.
//
// dump renders a File as the JSON Go's driver writes for --ast, an
// object of each node's fields keyed by Go's names, which the
// differential harness in tackc/porttest decodes and compares with Go's.
// Go's encoder refuses a value nested more than 10000 objects and arrays
// deep, and so does dump.
//
// BinaryOp, UnaryOp and Visibility are Go's named integer types, as
// structs wrapping a number, since Tack declares no named integer types;
// each value is a global, numbered as Go's constant is.
